<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sang Hongyang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 152–80 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sang Hongyang was an official of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> who served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> and Emperor Zhao, known for his economic policies. Born to a merchant family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and noted for his skill in mathematics, he became a Palace Attendant at 13. When the campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> had drained the treasury, he helped plan the state monopolies over iron and salt proposed in 120 BCE, systems later imitated by other dynasties, and as Assistant Minister of Agriculture introduced an asset tax on artisans, bankers and merchants. He became Imperial Secretary in 87 BCE and took part in the debate on Salt and Iron of 81 BCE, but was executed in 80 BCE by the regent Huo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-people">Guang</a> on charges of treason.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sang-hongyang.jpg" alt="Sang Hongyang" width="330" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sang_Hongyang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
